 \begin{abstract}
-The spread of \emph{agentic} artificial intelligence has outpaced the conceptual tools for characterizing agency in computational systems. Prevailing definitions rely on autonomy and goal-directedness. We argue for a minimal notion built on three criteria: intentionality as action grounded in beliefs and desires, rationality as action that follows coherently from a model of the world, and explainability as action causally traceable to internal states. We implement these criteria in an active inference agent acting in a partially observable Markov decision process, a task in which the agent cannot directly see the state of the world. In this agent, beliefs, preferences and the minimization of expected free energy form a chain that leads to action. We compare this implementation with belief--desire--intention architectures and model-based reinforcement learning under the same criteria. In a minimal T-maze, we measure control with empowerment, the maximum amount of information an agent's actions can carry into its next observations. Intentional action requires this kind of control. Structural manipulations of the generative model yield zero-, intermediate- and maximal-control regimes. We prove that learning about the environment never lowers the agent's own expected estimate of its empowerment, as long as its available actions and observations stay the same. An action taken to gain information therefore raises empowerment exactly when it leaves those actions and observations in place. Minimizing expected free energy selects that action by two separate routes. Preferences select it when the alternative outcomes are aversive or when knowing the answer helps a later decision. The drive to gain information selects it when that action is the more reliable source of the same information. Empowerment is never itself an objective. Empowerment measures controllability, and the paper keeps it separate from agency. In a companion pipeline, tensor networks (factorized probabilist