ic models) learn the generative model from behavior. They reproduce the control regimes across random seeds and on held-out data. Structure learning, using an observable fidelity that removes the gauge and amplitude phases which defeat the usual fidelity, together with Bayesian model reduction, shows that the hidden-state space we had specified by hand was wrong. In every seed it finds a smaller one, four states instead of six in the minimal maze and seven instead of eight in the pymdp T-maze, which is the smallest state space that generates the data. Which hidden variable an action is informative about cannot be identified from behavior, but the change in empowerment it produces can: the Cue and the arms gain the same information and have opposite effects on control. We close with a research agenda of falsifiable governance hypotheses.
