+The worked example manipulates hand-specified models. Phenotyping proper requires fitting a generative model to behavioral data and reading agency-relevant quantities off it. We report the first step. A matrix product state (MPS), a tensor network that represents a joint probability distribution as a chain of small tensors, is trained without supervision on T-maze rollouts~\citep{wauthierLearningGenerative2022}, so that $p(o,a)$ is learned and no longer written down by hand. Exact marginalisation of the learned model then gives, in closed form, the conditionals the empowerment probe needs and each action's information gain. Belief-like states are read off the bond, the internal index that links neighbouring tensors. The usual tool, the fidelity $|\langle b_i \mid b_j \rangle|^2$ between the bond vectors of two histories, depends on the phases of the amplitudes, which training on Born probabilities leaves unconstrained. We replace it with an \emph{observable fidelity}, which compares histories through the bond density matrix $b\,b^\dagger$ restricted to the subspace that the rest of the network can observe. It depends neither on the gauge, the arbitrary internal coordinate system of the MPS~\citep{aizpuruaTensorExplainable2024,ranTensorNetworkInterpretable2023}, nor on the phases. Two histories coincide under it exactly when they predict the same future, which is the criterion of predictive-state representations. The pipeline never sees the generative model, and this answers the circularity worry of Section~\ref{sec:priorwork}.
 
-\paragraph{Protocol.} We run the pipeline on the minimal maze of this paper and on the full pymdp T-maze, whose 24 composite observations (position, reward, cue context) make the analytic empowerment at the start 2 bits over the full observation space and 1 bit over the reward modality alone. For each seed we sample $N$ trajectories from the true process under a uniform-action policy ($N = 900$ for the minimal maze, $N = 4096$ for the full maze), hold out 20 percent of them, and train on the rest with the cumulant stochastic gradient descent of the MPS package (the bond dimension sets the size of the bond and so the capacity of the model; minimal maze: bond dimension 2, truncation cutoff 0.01, 40 epochs; full maze: bond dimension 4 growing to at most 16, 40 epochs, learning rate 0.005 with a schedule that shrinks it whenever the loss rises). The seed fixes the sample, the split and the initialisation. We report the $L_1$ distance from the learned joint to the analytic joint and to the held-out empirical joint, with the analytic-to-held-out distance as the sampling floor; the held-out negative log-likelihood per trajectory against the entropy of the true process; empowerment at the start, in a trap and after the cue; and the recovered state structure. Each action--observation history induces a vector, called a ray, on the bond of the MPS. The fidelity $|\langle b_i \mid b_j \rangle|^2$ between two rays measures how similar they are and does not depend on the gauge. We merge rays greedily when their fidelity exceeds 0.5 and take the number of clusters as the state count. We then compare the next-observation conditional carried by each history with the analytic one by total variation. We use five seeds for the minimal maze and three for the full maze. Code and per-seed results: \url{https://github.com/philipollaswilson/Tensor-Networks-for-the-Tmaze}, script \texttt{src/seed\_protocol.py}.
+\paragraph{Protocol.} We run the pipeline on the minimal maze of this paper and on the full pymdp T-maze, whose 24 composite observations (position, reward, cue context) make the analytic empowerment at the start 2 bits over the full observation space and 1 bit over the reward modality alone. For each seed we sample $N$ trajectories from the true process under a uniform-action policy ($N = 900$ for the minimal maze, $N = 4096$ for the full maze), hold out 20 percent of them, and train on the rest with the cumulant stochastic gradient descent of the MPS package (the bond dimension sets the size of the bond and so the capacity of the model; minimal maze: bond dimension 2, truncation cutoff 0.01, 40 epochs; full maze: bond dimension 4 growing to at most 16, 40 epochs, learning rate 0.005 with a schedule that shrinks it whenever the loss rises). The seed fixes the sample, the split and the initialisation. We report the $L_1$ distance from the learned joint to the analytic joint and to the held-out empirical joint, with the analytic-to-held-out distance as the sampling floor; the held-out negative log-likelihood per trajectory against the entropy of the true process; empowerment at the start, in a trap and after the cue; and the recovered state structure. Each action--observation history $h$ up to the bond induces a bond vector $b_h$. Every future Born probability is linear in $\rho_h = b_h b_h^\dagger$, so the distance $d(i,j)^2 = \sum_f \big(p(f \mid h_i) - p(f \mid h_j)\big)^2$ between the predicted futures of two histories is a quadratic form in $\rho_i - \rho_j$. Its kernel is a four-copy contraction of the network to the right of the bond, computed without enumerating futures and at a cost polynomial in the bond dimension. This distance is the observable fidelity; we condition it on each future action sequence. To count states without a distance threshold we use Bayesian model reduction. Each candidate state carries, for every next action, a Dirichlet--categorical model of the next observation, whose counts are the learned model's predictions weighted by the number of training visits to the history. Two candidates are merged when one shared Dirichlet has higher model evidence than two separate ones, greedily, until no merge raises the evidence. No distance threshold is set. The only free choice is the Dirichlet concentration $\alpha$, and we report the range of $\alpha$ over which the count is stable. In the full maze the predictions are over position and reward, with the context modality marginalised. For comparison we also cluster the bond vectors by the plain fidelity, merging when it exceeds 0.5. We then compare the next-observation conditional carried by each history with the analytic one by total variation. We use five seeds for the minimal maze and three for the full maze. Code and per-seed results: \url{https://github.com/philipollaswilson/Tensor-Networks-for-the-Tmaze}, script \texttt{src/seed\_protocol.py}.
 
-\paragraph{Results.} Table~\ref{tab:learned} gives means and standard deviations over seeds. On the minimal maze the learned joint is closer to the analytic joint than the held-out sample is ($L_1$ 0.218 $\pm$ 0.025 against a sampling floor of 0.240), the held-out log-likelihood is within 0.019 nats per trajectory of the entropy of the true process (2.890 nats), and the three empowerment values are recovered to three decimals in every seed. Fidelity clustering returns the four hidden states (cheese-trap, shock-trap, one per cue context) in 3 of 5 seeds. In the other two seeds the two histories that enter the shock trap carry the same next-observation conditional (total variation below $10^{-12}$ in every seed) but occupy different rays, so fidelity clustering splits that trap into two states; a criterion that merges histories making identical predictions (predictive equivalence) merges them. On the full maze the learned joint is again closer to the analytic joint than the held-out sample is ($L_1$ 0.193 $\pm$ 0.011 against a floor of 0.396), the held-out log-likelihood is within 0.032 nats of the entropy (5.242 nats), the start and post-cue values are recovered over both observation spaces, the trap value is within 0.01 bits of zero, and fidelity clustering returns the seven hidden states (unresolved centre, four arm--outcome traps, two cue contexts) in 2 of 3 seeds, with a maximum total variation of 0.049 between the learned and analytic reward conditionals of any history. In the third seed the unresolved centre and one cue context overlap with fidelity 0.55 and the 0.5 threshold merges them; their reward conditionals differ, so predictive equivalence separates them. Figure~\ref{fig:fidelity} shows the seed-0 fidelity matrices. The residual $L_1$ to the analytic joint is below the sampling floor on both mazes, so the sample limits the fit before the model does.
+\paragraph{Main result: structure learning corrects the hand-specified state space.} Our hand-specified models, like pymdp's, split the states reached after the first move by the context, and the minimal maze also by the arm of entry. That gives six states in the minimal maze and eight in the pymdp T-maze. Trained on behavior alone, the tensor networks show in every seed that several of these distinctions are not supported by behavior (Fig.~\ref{fig:states}). In the minimal maze they find four states: cheese-trap, shock-trap, and one per cue context. The arm of entry, and with it the context behind each trap, makes no difference to any later observation. In the pymdp T-maze they find seven: the unresolved centre, four arm--outcome traps and two cue contexts. The two context-specific centre states merge, because no observation separates them before the Cue is read. In each maze the learned state space is the smallest that generates the data. It is therefore the hidden-state space that behavior supports, and ours was finer than the evidence allows. Under the observable fidelity, histories of one state lie at most 0.17 apart and different states at least 0.42 apart (minimal maze: 0.00 and 1.15). Bayesian model reduction returns these counts with no threshold, for every $\alpha$ from $10^{-3}$ to 2 in the minimal maze and from $10^{-3}$ to 0.5 in the full maze. At $\alpha = 0.1$ every accepted merge raises the log evidence by at least 7 nats in the minimal maze and 20 in the full maze, and every refused merge would lower it by more than 120 and 65 nats. The plain fidelity does not achieve this. It returns four states in only 3 of 5 minimal-maze seeds and 13, 10 and 11 states over the 24 full-maze histories (Table~\ref{tab:learned}).
+
+\paragraph{Fit, control and the limits of the state count.} Table~\ref{tab:learned} gives means and standard deviations over seeds. On the minimal maze the learned joint is closer to the analytic joint than the held-out sample is ($L_1$ 0.218 $\pm$ 0.025 against a sampling floor of 0.240), the held-out log-likelihood is within 0.019 nats per trajectory of the entropy of the true process (2.890 nats), and the three empowerment values are recovered to three decimals in every seed. In the two seeds where the plain fidelity fails, the histories that enter the shock trap make the same predictions (total variation below $10^{-12}$) but occupy different bond directions, and fidelity splits that trap in two. On the full maze the learned joint is again closer to the analytic joint than the held-out sample is ($L_1$ 0.193 $\pm$ 0.011 against a floor of 0.396), the held-out log-likelihood is within 0.032 nats of the entropy (5.242 nats), the start and post-cue values are recovered over both observation spaces, and the trap value is within 0.01 bits of zero. Every recovered state except the centre predicts the analytic next-observation distribution to within 0.002 in total variation, in every seed and on both mazes. The centre deviates by 0.11 to 0.17, the sampling noise of its training counts. A dense prior ($\alpha \ge 1$) spreads its mass over twelve position--reward outcomes per action, of which at most two occur. This inflates the cost of keeping a state and merges the centre with a cue context, so near-deterministic outcomes call for a sparse prior. Marginalising position as well gives five states in every seed, because the arms of entry then merge by outcome as in the minimal maze. The state count is therefore relative to the modalities the agent observes. Histories within one state differ most at the centre, by the sampling noise of the training counts (about 50 rollouts per history and action), which the model reproduces; the evidence weighs these differences against the sample size. Over seven hand-picked histories, one per expected state, the plain fidelity returns 7, 7 and 6 states. The fidelity between the histories' future wavefunctions, which removes any dependence on the gauge, gives the same counts as the plain fidelity, so its failure comes from the amplitude phases that the observable fidelity discards. The residual $L_1$ to the analytic joint is below the sampling floor on both mazes, so the sample limits the fit before the model does.
+
+\paragraph{Implication for the main hypothesis.} The learned models confirm the empowerment signature behind Proposition~\ref{prop:epistemic}. Its explanation as learning about the context, however, turns out to be a choice we made when specifying the state space by hand, and behavior cannot certify it. The learned states are predictive states. They are the coarsest partition of histories that preserves every prediction, and in this two-step task they form the smallest state space at the bond that generates the data. The specified model of Section~\ref{sec:efe} distinguishes the choice point with the cheese on the left from the choice point with the cheese on the right. The learned model merges them, since no observation can tell them apart until the Cue is read. The same identifiability limit applies to information gain. The context model of Section~\ref{sec:efe} and a model in which an arm draws a fair outcome coin generate identical data, and they disagree on whether entering an arm is informative about the context. What the learned model does fix is that every action at the choice point resolves one bit about the next predictive state, either cheese-trap against shock-trap or one cue context against the other. It also fixes the net change in empowerment each action produces, $+0.58$ bits for the Cue and $-1$ bit for either arm, in every seed. The decomposition of Proposition~\ref{prop:epistemic} into an information term and an interface term depends on which latent variable is chosen, so it is a property of the specified model and not of behavior; its sum is identifiable. For phenotyping, the signature of interface preservation can be read off a learned model: equal information gain with opposite changes in empowerment. Attributing that signature to learning about the context cannot be read off it.
 
 The learned models phenotype control. They recover the channel and its latent structure. They do not recover the preferences or the causal chain from beliefs to action that the criteria of Section~\ref{sec:definition} require, so the learned-model results are control phenotyping. Agency phenotyping proper needs inverse inference of preferences, which is future work. The pipeline also identifies the interventional channel $p(o_{t+1} \mid \mathrm{do}(a_t))$ from observational data only under a stated condition, that the rollouts give every action full support at every reachable state, chosen independently of the system's own policy. Our rollouts are sampled under a uniform-action policy, so the condition holds. For data generated by a competent policy it fails, since such a policy never takes the uninformative actions whose consequences the channel needs; the companion repository includes a check, computed from the data alone, of whether the condition holds.
 
