 \subsection{Conclusion}
 
-The contribution of this paper is mainly conceptual. It gives a three-criterion definition of agency (intentionality, rationality, explainability) that is grounded in philosophy and can be operationalized. It argues that active inference realizes all three criteria together under a single normative objective, in a way that can be compared across architectures (Table~\ref{tab:frameworks}). It also gives a controlled illustration in a minimal POMDP. There, empowerment, used strictly as a probe of controllability, tracks structural changes to the agentic action chain. Epistemic actions that keep the interface intact increase empowerment, and expected free energy minimization selects such actions without empowerment being an explicit objective. It does so through preferences in the deterministic maze, and through the epistemic term as soon as the epistemic action is the more reliable source of the context. Whether this becomes phenotyping in the full sense depends on the program of Section~\ref{sec:learned}, which fits generative models to the behavior of real AI systems and checks the inferred agency profiles against the interventions of H1--H3.
+The contribution of this paper is mainly conceptual. It gives a three-criterion definition of agency (intentionality, rationality, explainability) that is grounded in philosophy and can be operationalized. It argues that active inference realizes all three criteria together under a single normative objective, in a way that can be compared across architectures (Table~\ref{tab:frameworks}). It also gives a controlled illustration in a minimal POMDP. There, empowerment, used strictly as a probe of controllability, tracks structural changes to the agentic action chain. Epistemic actions that keep the interface intact increase empowerment, and expected free energy minimization selects such actions without empowerment being an explicit objective. It does so through preferences in the deterministic maze, and through the epistemic term as soon as the epistemic action is the more reliable source of the context. Tensor networks trained on behavior alone, using an observable fidelity and Bayesian model reduction, show that the hidden-state space we specified by hand was finer than behavior supports, and they identify the smallest one that generates the data. They confirm the signature of interface preservation: equal information gain about the next state with opposite changes in empowerment. They also show that attributing this signature to learning about the context is a property of the specified state space, and behavior cannot certify it. Whether this becomes phenotyping in the full sense depends on the program of Section~\ref{sec:learned}, which fits generative models to the behavior of real AI systems and checks the inferred agency profiles against the interventions of H1--H3.
 
